<TopicPage slug="subjects/film/advanced/documentary-form">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Documentary is <strong>the negotiation between reality and form</strong> — real people, real
    stakes, shaped by cinematic grammar. Its modes, its ethics and its impact history make it
    the branch of film where the theory lesson\'s questions are least avoidable.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="ethics as form, the modes, objectivity-as-style, impact" widget="equationStep" config={docCfg} />

  <h2>Formal landmarks</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Nanook of the North (1922):</strong> staged hunts, a "wife" who was not his wife — the founding fiction that defined documentary\'s original sin and its reflexive self-awareness ever since (the history lesson\'s sources-and-evidence discipline, filmmaking edition).</li>
    <li><strong>Direct cinema vs cinéma vérité:</strong> American (Drew Associates: primary cameras, no questions) vs French (Rouch: provoke and participate) — two honest answers to "should the filmmaker intervene?"</li>
    <li><strong>The essay film:</strong> from Sans Soleil to modern video essays — the thinking person\'s form, where narration and image argue (the literature lesson\'s close reading, audiovisual edition).</li>
    <li><strong>Streaming changed the economics:</strong> doc series (Making a Murderer, The Last Dance) brought long-form budgets and true-crime\'s ethics problem — entertainment pressure on real lives (the journalism lesson\'s tension, industrialised).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Nichols, Introduction to Documentary; Barnouw, Documentary: A History of the Non-Fiction Film; the ethics lessons.</p>
</TopicPage>
